On Fri, Feb 1, 2013 at 3:30 PM, Judson Lester <nyarly@gmail.com> wrote:

> On Fri, Feb 1, 2013 at 12:43 PM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>>  What would be the problem with "do jenca mi"?  I suppose there's some
>>> elided abstraction that I could use in the x1 place to mean "some event
>>> related to you" - but why would that be neccesary?
>>>
>>
>> Simply because the English "You shocked me!" actually means "What you
>> just did shocked me!". This has to do with the issue "sumti raising", which
>> has been bandied about countless times throughout the years.
>>
>
> I've got two follow on questions, then:
>
>
>    1. The easy one: "tu'a do jenca mi" would be semantically valid,
>    though?  Maybe a little vague, but if you've just punched me in the face,
>    maybe clear enough and readier to hand than "le nu do darxi fo le mi flira
>    cu jenca mi"
>
> Yes, that's correct. In fact, the use of tu'a and jai as regards sumti
raising is talked about here: http://dag.github.com/cll/11/10/

>
>    1. More philosophical: granted that it doesn't make *sense* most of
>    the time that do jenca - because people aren't shocking - why isn't the
>    convention that such places raise their sumti automatically?  Or is the
>    idea that maybe there could be a brain-interacting object that fills the
>    space?
>
> Ian answered this one brilliantly, so I'll defer to him.

>
>
> Come to think, would it be reasonable to say something like "lo sidbo
> jenca mi" or would it still need an abstraction related to 'lo nu sidbo fi
> mi' ?  #2 in play as a "why not?" if not.
>

Yes, but you need to close the sumti: {lo sidbo [ku/cu] jenca mi}, else it
parses as "An idea type-of shocker, me", with no selbri at all.


>
> Judson
